<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Quantification of Understanding</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4. Result and Discussion ‣ Does Language Model Understand Language?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> provides a detailed breakdown of model performance across three linguistic categories: English, Bengali, and Mixed (cross-lingual) sentence pairs. The evaluation is based on four complementary metrics: Pearson <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahmed and Kumar, 2018</a>)</cite> and Spearman <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zar, 2005</a>)</cite> correlations, which measure alignment with human similarity judgments; Mean Absolute Error (MAE), indicating prediction precision; and the Human Calibration Envelope (HCE) Accuracy, a linguistically motivated metric quantifying the frequency with which model predictions fall within one standard deviation of the mean human rating—thus capturing tolerance for intersubjective variation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2. </span>Average reasoning quality scores across prompt categories for different models, where <span id="S4.T2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Same Tense Different Voice (STDV)</span>, <span id="S4.T2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Different Tense Different Voice (DTDV)</span>, <span id="S4.T2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Different Tense Same Voice (DTSV)</span>, <span id="S4.T2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Negation- Affirmative (NA)</span>.</figcaption>
